      "functional_impact_measured": "Surface labeling confirmed by multiple assays. Receptor internalization assays performed with N-terminally tagged constructs (L8, L9, L10), confirming functional trafficking. Cell-impermeant SNAP probe confirms plasma membrane surface display (L14). GIP-stimulated signaling and trafficking preserved.",
      "confidence": "high",
      "rationale": "The signal peptide spans residues 1-21 (Q at position 21 is the last SP residue); the mature N-terminus begins at R22. L6 explicitly states the myc tag was placed 'immediately downstream of the predicted signal peptide', which by topology uniquely resolves to insert_after_residue=21 (between Q21 and R22). This is the only extracellular N-terminal position available on GIPR, confirmed by at least six independent tagged constructs across multiple labs (L6, L8, L9, L13, L15, L17). The 115-residue extracellular domain (22-136) provides ample space for a short epitope tag, and internalization assays confirm functional receptor trafficking is preserved. Full-length human GIPR with myc tag introduced immediately downstream of the predicted signal peptide (SP residues 1-21), expressed in HEK cells for surface labeling. Multiple independent studies (L8: 3X-HA+EGFP for internalization; L9/L17: N-terminal SNAP for real-time internalization; L10: SNAP-GIPR in INS-1 beta cells for trafficking; L13: SNAPf replacing SP for surface expression; L15: FLAG+mNG at N-terminus) all place tags at this same extracellular N-terminal position.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "pmid": 25191754,
          "citation": "PMID 25191754",
          "claim": "Therefore, to label the receptors at their N-termini, a myc-tag was introduced immediately downstream of the predicted signal peptide."
        }
      ],
